    "version": "1",
    "body": "## Introduction\n\nMUVE Futures is committed to providing a welcoming, inclusive, and person-centred admissions process that recognises the individuality of every learner. As a specialist Alternative Provision supporting children, young people, and young adults aged 4 to 25, we understand that many referrals come at times of significant challenge, uncertainty, or transition for learners and their families. Our admissions process is designed to be supportive, responsive, and focused on understanding the whole person rather than simply reviewing documentation or previous educational experiences.\n\nMUVE Futures supports learners with a wide range of needs, including SEND (with or without an EHCP), SEMH needs, autism, ADHD, anxiety, Emotionally Based Non-School Attendance (EBNSA), medical needs, Looked After Children, care leavers, and learners who are unable to access traditional educational settings.\n\n## Policy Statement\n\nMUVE Futures believes that every learner deserves access to an educational environment that understands their strengths, aspirations, barriers, and support needs. The admissions process is therefore designed to be collaborative, empathetic, and centred around identifying how the organisation can effectively support a learner to re-engage with education and make progress towards positive outcomes.\n\nWe recognise that no two learners are the same, and admissions decisions will be based on an individual assessment of need, suitability, risk, and the ability of MUVE Futures to provide a safe, effective, and personalised programme of support.\n\nReferral Process\n\nReferrals may be received from a variety of sources, including; Local Authorities, schools, Multi-Academy Trusts, SEND teams, parents /carers and Virtual Schools, social care teams, healthcare providers, residential services, and other commissioning organisations.\n\nFollowing receipt of a referral, MUVE Futures will review all available information, which may include:\n\n- Online referral form / expression of interest\n- If the learner has an EHCP, we will review this documentation.\n- Professional reports.\n- Educational records.\n- Safeguarding information.\n- Behaviour and risk assessments.\n- Attendance information.\n- Medical information.\n- Social care involvement.\n- Previous placement history.\n\nThis information allows MUVE Futures to gain a holistic understanding of the learner and determine whether the provision can meet their educational, emotional, social, and wellbeing needs safely and effectively.\n\n## Person-Centred Assessment\n\nThe admissions process is not solely based on paperwork. Wherever possible, MUVE Futures will seek to meet with the learner, family, carers, and relevant professionals before a placement begins.\n\nWe recognise that many learners have experienced challenges within education and may feel anxious about starting a new provision. Our approach is therefore designed to be calm, supportive, and strengths-based, allowing sufficient time to build trust and understand each learner’s preferences, aspirations, interests, communication style, and support requirements.\n\nConsideration will be given to academic needs, SEND requirements, EHCP outcomes, emotional wellbeing, social circumstances, safeguarding considerations, therapeutic recommendations, long-term goals, and future transition plans. We link our learners journey into careers advice and guidance tailored to their interests.\n\nMUVE Futures will then create a care or educational support plan which includes a wrap-around support journey, utilising our extensive experienced employees available to the learner.\n\n## Individual Support and Risk Assessment\n\nPrior to admission, MUVE Futures may undertake individual assessments and risk assessments to ensure that appropriate arrangements can be implemented from the outset.\n\nWhere required, personalised Support Plans, Behaviour Support Plans, Healthcare Plans, Attendance Plans, Fire Safety Plans, or Risk Assessments may be developed to ensure that support is tailored to the learner’s individual profile and circumstances.\n\nThis enables staff to understand identified risks, barriers to learning, safeguarding considerations, reasonable adjustments, and any specialist support that may be required for successful engagement. Where required we may use 2-1 staffing according to risk assessments and this will be reflected in our rates.\n\n## Equality, Inclusion and Accessibility\n\nMUVE Futures is committed to providing equality of opportunity for all learners and will not discriminate based on disability, special educational needs, race, religion, gender, sexual orientation, family circumstances, care experience, health needs, or social disadvantage. Whilst we appreciate our learners have various different protected characteristics, as a company we hold the same expectation towards the treatment of our employees.\n\nWe are committed to making reasonable adjustments wherever possible to ensure that learners can access provision safely, effectively, and with dignity. Individual plans, education and learning plans and therapeutic input is all tailored to each learners needs or EHCP targets.\n\nThe admissions process itself will be accessible and flexible, recognising that some learners and families may require additional support to engage fully in discussions and decision-making.\n\n## Placement Decisions\n\nFollowing assessment and consultation, MUVE Futures will determine whether the organisation is able to meet the learner’s needs safely and effectively within its Alternative Provision model.\n\nDecisions will consider:\n\n- The learner’s needs and desired outcomes.\n- Health and safety considerations.\n- Safeguarding requirements.\n- Staffing and resource availability.\n- Suitability of delivery environments.\n- Potential benefit to the learner.\n\nWhere a placement is accepted, a personalised transition plan will be developed to support a smooth start and positive engagement with provision.\n\nWhere MUVE Futures believes that another service would be more appropriate, this will be communicated openly with commissioners and families to ensure that the learner’s best interests remain at the centre of decision-making.\n\n## Transition into MUVE Futures\n\nMUVE Futures understands that transitions can be challenging, particularly for learners with SEND, anxiety, SEMH needs, previous negative educational experiences, or significant vulnerabilities.\n\nThe organisation is committed to ensuring that all placements begin through a structured and compassionate transition process. This may include introductory meetings, phased starts, home visits, community-based introductions, visits to learning environments, and multi-agency planning discussions.\n\nThe aim is to minimise anxiety, build positive relationships, and create the foundations for successful engagement and progress from the beginning of the placement.\n\n## Monitoring and Review\n\nRegional Alternative Provision Lead has overall responsibility for overseeing admissions and ensuring that admission decisions are fair, inclusive, transparent, and centred on the needs of each learner.\n\nMUVE Futures will regularly review its admissions procedures to ensure that they remain consistent with safeguarding requirements, SEND legislation, Alternative Provision best practice, and the organisation’s commitment to delivering high-quality personalised support.\n",
